{"subjectId":"c","id":"pointer-arithmetic","title":"Pointer Arithmetic","group":"Pointers","order":23,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Adding <code>n</code> to a pointer moves it by <code>n</code> <em>elements</em>, not bytes: <code>p + 1</code> advances by <code>sizeof *p</code>. Arithmetic is only valid within one array (plus one-past-the-end).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    int *p = a;                  /* &amp;a[0] */\n    char *c = (char *)a;\n\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", *p, *(p + 1), *(p + 3));\n    printf(\"int step  = %td bytes\\n\", (char *)(p + 1) - (char *)p);\n    printf(\"char step = %td bytes\\n\", (c + 1) - c);\n\n    int *end = a + 4;            /* one past the end: may be compared, never dereferenced */\n    printf(\"elements between = %td\\n\", end - p);\n    for (int *q = a; q &lt; end; q++) printf(\"%d \", *q);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 20 40\nint step  = 4 bytes\nchar step = 1 bytes\nelements between = 4\n10 20 30 40</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>What is allowed</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Allowed?</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>p + n</code>, <code>p - n</code>, <code>p++</code>, <code>p--</code></td><td>Yes, within the array / one past end</td><td>Pointer</td></tr><tr><td><code>p - q</code> (same array)</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>ptrdiff_t</code> element count (<code>%td</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>p &lt; q</code>, <code>p == q</code></td><td>Yes (relational only within one array)</td><td>0 / 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>p + q</code>, <code>p * 2</code>, <code>p / 2</code></td><td><strong>No</strong> — compile error</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>p + n</code> beyond one-past-end</td><td><strong>Undefined</strong> even without dereferencing</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Arithmetic on <code>void *</code></td><td>Not in standard C (GNU extension treats it as <code>char *</code>)</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Idioms</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nsize_t length(const char *s) {\n    const char *p = s;\n    while (*p) p++;                 /* walk to the terminator */\n    return (size_t)(p - s);         /* pointer difference = count */\n}\n\nvoid copy(char *dst, const char *src) {\n    while ((*dst++ = *src++) != '\\0') { }   /* classic K&amp;R strcpy */\n}\n\nint sum(const int *begin, const int *end) {\n    int s = 0;\n    while (begin != end) s += *begin++;     /* *(begin++) */\n    return s;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    char buf[16];\n    copy(buf, \"pointer\");\n    int v[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    printf(\"%s %zu %d\\n\", buf, length(buf), sum(v, v + 5));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>pointer 7 15</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Precedence of <code>*</code> and <code>++</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {10, 20, 30};\n    int *p = a;\n    int x;\n    x = *p++;    printf(\"*p++   -&gt; %d, now *p = %d\\n\", x, *p);   /* read, then move */\n    x = *++p;    printf(\"*++p   -&gt; %d\\n\", x);                    /* move, then read */\n    p = a;\n    x = ++*p;    printf(\"++*p   -&gt; %d, a[0] = %d\\n\", x, a[0]);   /* increment the value */\n    x = (*p)++;  printf(\"(*p)++ -&gt; %d, a[0] = %d\\n\", x, a[0]);   /* read, then increment value */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>*p++   -&gt; 10, now *p = 20\n*++p   -&gt; 30\n++*p   -&gt; 11, a[0] = 11\n(*p)++ -&gt; 11, a[0] = 12</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Walking off the end</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — heap-buffer-overflow caught by AddressSanitizer</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int *a = malloc(3 * sizeof *a);\n    if (!a) return 1;\n    for (int *p = a; p &lt;= a + 3; p++)   /* &lt;= : writes a[3] */\n        *p = 0;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a[0]);\n    free(a);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>If int *p = (int *)1000, what is p + 1?</summary><div><p><code>1004</code> on a platform with 4-byte <code>int</code> — pointer arithmetic scales by the size of the pointed-to type. For <code>char *</code> it would be <code>1001</code>, for <code>double *</code> <code>1008</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can you subtract two pointers? Add them?</summary><div><p>You can subtract two pointers into the same array: the result is the number of elements between them (<code>ptrdiff_t</code>). Adding two pointers is meaningless and a compile error.</p></div></details></section>"}